            "review_text": "Andy N\n2 contributions\n0\nAll bones, scammers\nApr 2017\nWife and her friends wanted a simple Hainan chicken rice dinner. According to the menu, the prices were really cheap so we ordered extras (soup/canh, stir fried greens/rau muon). For mains we ordered two plates of hainan chicken (40,000 each), one plate of roast duck (40,000), BBQ pork (35,000). \n\nBefore the waitress leaves, she pulls out the scam. They convince you to put it all on a combination plate (thập cẩm). We agreed since they said the cost would be the same or less. This actually changes the line item charge in the point of sale, and gets harder to argue about later when you get the bill. \n\nSo we get our food as ordered... two quarter portions of chicken (one white and one dark) smothered / completely covered in onions so you can't actually see how much chicken you got, one portion each of BBQ pork and roast duck maybe 50 grams each. When we get the bill, the chicken is now a charge for a whole chicken plate 160,000 plus 40,000 for the bowl of rice (rice is part of the 40,000 chicken plate). The BBQ pork and roast duck are under a meat combo charge of 150,000, which is the price for like a quarter kilo. Tried to argue, but they insisted that they charged us for what we got. We actually got half of what we asked for. Got tired of the obviously well versed scam and just left.\nRead more\nWritten April 3, 2017",
